<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#uparaja">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uparaja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uparat, Ouparach, Maha Uparaja, Ainshe Min, Front Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uparaja, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> upa-rāja, vice king, was a noble title for the viceroy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the viceroy of the Magadha Empire bore it, and in the Buddhist dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and some of their tributary kingdoms. In Burma the Maha Uparaja, or Ainshe Min, was the highest rank among the princes of the royal blood, though Europeans wrongly took it for crown prince: it carried no sure promise of succession, which was usually settled by a struggle for power. In Siam the Uparat, more often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a> or Second King, was discontinued in 1876 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> after the Front Palace Crisis of 1874, in favour of a crown prince.</p></div>
